  <h2>Why do we celebrate Easwaramma Day?</h2>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/img/easwaramma/easwaramma-with-swami.webp"
      alt="A sepia photograph of Mother Easwaramma with Bhagawan Sri Sathya Sai Baba"
      width="1000"
      height="780"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
  </figure>
  <p>
    May 6th every year is celebrated as Easwaramma Day, commemorating the anniversary of the passing away of the saintly
    lady who was blessed to be mother to the Avatar of the Kali age. Bhagawan exhorts His devotees, especially children,
    to revere the mother as God. Hence, this day is a celebration of the sacred ideal of motherhood.
  </p>
  <p>
    Easwaramma was very fond of children and was one of the pioneers of the Bal Vikas movement. Hence, this day is also
    celebrated as Bal Vikas Day, when hundreds of Bal Vikas children gather in Bhagawan’s presence to present before Him
    their cultural programmes and imbibe the highest spiritual lessons from their Divine Mother.
    <a href="/easwaramma-day/">See photographs from the 2026 celebration</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Additional seva opportunities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Our Narayan seva at the Austin Street shelter in Dallas, every first Friday of a month.</li>
    <li>
      On the first Saturday of every month from 10 am, the Shirdi Baba temple in Plano organises a Cleaning Seva. The
      temple has been home for our unit presentations. Children are encouraged to participate as an expression of
      gratitude.
    </li>
    <li>Breakfast Seva, serving healthy and nutritious breakfast to the homeless in Dallas every day, led by one of our own teachers.</li>
    <li>Volunteers can also collect our white sheets once every other month and return them washed. Parking volunteers could also use your help.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Want to get involved? Talk to your child’s teacher.</p>
